<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shoen">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shōen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as shōen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A shōen was a private estate or manor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from about the 8th to the 15th century, often tax-free and autonomous, whose rise undermined the power of the emperor. The estates grew from lands assigned to shrines and temples or granted by the emperor, and from fields reclaimed under laws of 723 and 743 that let reclaimers keep them, eroding the ritsuryō system. Peasants commended their land to temples, shrines and court nobles to escape taxes, and by the end of the Heian period virtually all land in Japan was shōen. The estates fostered a local military class; stewards appointed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> weakened their lords, and the system ended as villages came under daimyō.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shoen.jpg" alt="Shōen" width="330" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nukata-dera_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
